<body>
<h1 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 16.1px 0.0px; font: 24.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #138756; -webkit-text-stroke: #138756"><span class="s1"><b>How to Read a Contractor Quote (Without Getting Gouged)</b></span></h1>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><i>A line-by-line guide to understanding what you're actually paying for</i></span></p>
<p class="p3"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">You've got a contractor quote in hand. It's a mess of numbers, materials you've never heard of, and a total at the bottom that makes you wince.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Is it fair? Is it high? How would you even know?</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">This guide walks you through reading a contractor quote like someone who's seen thousands of them.</span></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>The Anatomy of a Good Quote</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">A professional quote should have these sections:</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>1. Scope of Work</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">This describes WHAT they're doing. Not just "roof replacement" — specifics: - Tear off existing shingles (1 layer or 2?) - Install ice &amp; water shield at eaves and valleys - Install synthetic underlayment - Install 30-year architectural shingles - Replace all flashing, drip edge, and ridge vents - Cleanup and disposal</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Red flag:</b> Vague scope like "complete roof job" with no details. This is how change orders happen.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>2. Materials Section</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Should list: - Product names and types (not just "shingles") - Quantities (squares for roofing, square feet for flooring) - Unit prices</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Example:</span></p>
<p class="p6"><span class="s1">Architectural shingles (GAF Timberline HDZ): 24 squares @ $120/sq = $2,880</span></p>
<p class="p6"><span class="s1">Synthetic underlayment: 24 squares @ $15/sq = $360</span></p>
<p class="p6"><span class="s1">Ice &amp; water shield: 5 squares @ $35/sq = $175</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Red flag:</b> No quantities or unit prices. Just "materials: $5,000." You have no idea if they're using quality products or marking up cheap ones.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>3. Labor Section</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Should show: - What tasks are included - Either hours/rates OR flat labor cost by task</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Example:</span></p>
<p class="p6"><span class="s1">Tear-off and prep: $1,800</span></p>
<p class="p6"><span class="s1">Installation: $3,800</span></p>
<p class="p6"><span class="s1">Cleanup and disposal: $500</span></p>
<p class="p6"><span class="s1">Total labor: $6,100</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Red flag:</b> No labor breakdown at all. "Labor included" hides whether they're padding this line.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>4. Additional Items</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Things like: - Permits (if required) - Dumpster rental - Equipment rental - Travel/delivery fees</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">These should be itemized, not buried.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>5. Payment Terms</b></span></h3>
<ul class="ul1">
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"></span><span class="s1">Deposit amount (should be 10-33%, NOT 50%+)</span></li>
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"></span><span class="s1">Progress payments</span></li>
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"></span><span class="s1">Final payment terms</span></li>
</ul>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Red flag:</b> 50% or more upfront. Reputable contractors don't need half your money before starting.</span></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>The Math Check</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Want to sanity-check a quote? Here's a rough formula:</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Materials cost × 2.5 to 3.5 = Total project cost</b></span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">This accounts for labor, overhead, and profit. So if materials legitimately cost $4,000, expect a total quote of $10,000-14,000.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">If they're quoting $20,000? Ask questions.</span></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>What's Missing = What You'll Pay Later</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Common items contractors "forget" to include:</span></p>
<ul class="ul1">
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Permit costs</b> — often $200-500, legally required</span></li>
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Disposal fees</b> — dumpsters cost $400-600</span></li>
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Code-required upgrades</b> — discovered "during the job"</span></li>
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Flashing and trim work</b> — the details that make it watertight</span></li>
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"><b></b></span><span class="s1"><b>Site prep and cleanup</b> — assumed, but not always included</span></li>
</ul>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Get clarification on all of these BEFORE signing.</span></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Questions to Ask</b></span></h2>
<ol class="ol1">
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"></span><span class="s1">"Can you break out materials and labor separately?"</span></li>
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"></span><span class="s1">"What's your markup on materials?" (15-30% is normal)</span></li>
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"></span><span class="s1">"What happens if you find damage behind the [siding/shingles/drywall]?"</span></li>
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"></span><span class="s1">"Is this quote good for 30/60/90 days?"</span></li>
  <li class="li7"><span class="s2"></span><span class="s1">"What's NOT included that I should know about?"</span></li>
</ol>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">A legitimate contractor will answer these without getting defensive.</span></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>The Bottom Line</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">A good quote is transparent. You should be able to trace where every dollar goes.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">If a quote is vague, rushed, or pressured — that's a red flag bigger than the price itself.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">And if you want a second opinion on whether your quote is fair? That's exactly what we're building.</span></p>
<p class="p3"><span class="s1"></span><br></p>
